  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nAt noon, scattered showers were being observed especially north of \nSan Diego County. Rainfall totals over the past 6 hours have seen \nquite the range with accumulations of 0.01 to 0.10\" and up to \n0.60 to 1\". Highest totals have been observed near mountain \nfoothills and in the mountains. Isolated thunderstorms are \nexpected to continue through the afternoon. Rainfall activity is \nexpected move south more into San Diego County through this \nafternoon and evening, but will remain scattered in coverage. For \nthis evening and overnight peak rainfall rates of 0.25 to 0.35 \ninches per hour are expected. An additional 0.25 to 0.75 inch of \nrain may accumulate in some locations. In addition to periods of \nrain, gusty winds can be expected over the mountains, locally into\nthe deserts. Peak gusts of 45 to 55 mph are expected through this\nevening. \n\nHigh temperatures on Friday will be a few degrees warmer than today \nfor the coast to the mountains with greater warming of 5 to 10 \ndegrees on Saturday. High temperatures on Saturday will be as much \nas 5 to 10 degrees below average for the mountains. Conditions will \ncontinue to warm Sunday and Monday. By Monday, high temperatures in \ncoastal areas will be 5 to 10 degrees above average, with \ntemperatures closer to average further inland. \n\nThe flow will turn offshore for the weekend, with periods of north \nto northeasterly winds in the mornings. Strongest winds will be on \nthe mountain foothills and below passes and canyons. Right now it \nlooks like peak wind gusts will be in the 30 to 45 mph range. Given \nthe unsettled weather quickly turning to offshore flow, marine layer \nlow clouds and fog will largely be absent from the coast into early \nnext week.",
